  "query_preview": "..\nAn upper trough has moved into the western states with low\npressure at the surface and at mid-levels bringing a band of\nprecipitation east through central Montana this morning. As this\nprecipitation moves through the CWA toward the North Dakota \nborder today (Wednesday), temperatures should be warm enough for \nlower elevations to experience rain. But colder air will move in \non the backside to allow it to mix mostly at higher elevations \nbefore ending later today or tonight. The greatest accumulation is\nexpected over SW and NW Phillips county with about 1 to 2 inches.\nWill leave the Winter Weather Advisory going in SW Phillips but \nthere looks to be little if any impact to Highway 191.\n\nIn the meantime, dense fog is a possibility into this morning \nover mainly the southeast zones. The cold front that arrives by \nafternoon should disperse this quickly.\n\nNW winds on the backside of this system will kick up hazardous \nconditions on Fort Peck Lake today. The current Lake Wind Advisory\nlooks good.\n\nPrecipitation is expected to gradually end tonight over the NE\nzones. Temps look to stay just warm enough that accumulating snow\nshouldn't be too much of a concern.\n\nLarge scale upper troughing continues across the\nwestern US the remainder of the the week. The next system will \nbring another shot of colder air from the north this weekend. \nSome light snow is possible but the main impact will be the below \nnormal temps. Some slow modifying can be expected early next week\nas the upper flow gradually becomes more zonal.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nDid not make too many tweaks to inherited forecast from the NBM.\n\n- Confidence in temperatures is moderate to high through the\nweekend.\n\n- Confidence in the snowfall forecast has become consistent and\n  therefore is moderate. TFJ",
